# Copyright for new functionality modules

When adding a **new functionality module** (new `.cpp` / `.h` / `.hpp` / `.cc` that introduces feature code, not a one-line stub), put a copyright line in the file header using the same style as nearby authors in that tree.

## Required line (current year)

```text
Copyright (C) 2026 Peter Adrianov <peter.adrianov@gmail.com>
```

Use the **current calendar year** when creating the file (replace `2026` if the year has changed).

## Format by tree

**dcpp / dht** (`/*` + `* ` lines, often with Jacek Sieka / EiskaltDC++ / Boris Pek):

```c
/*
 * Copyright (C) 2001-2012 Jacek Sieka, arnetheduck on gmail point com
 * Copyright (C) 2026 Peter Adrianov <peter.adrianov@gmail.com>
 *
 * This program is free software; ...
 */
```

Append Peter’s line after existing `Copyright` lines; do not remove prior authors.

**eiskaltdcpp-qt** (`/*` block or `/***` banner):

```c
/*
 * Copyright (C) 2026 Peter Adrianov <peter.adrianov@gmail.com>
 *
 * This program is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify
 * it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
 * the Free Software Foundation; either version 3 of the License, or
 * (at your option) any later version.
 */
```

Or inside a `/***` banner, pad to the same width as neighboring star lines (usually 73 chars between `*`):

```c
*   Copyright (C) 2026 Peter Adrianov <peter.adrianov@gmail.com>          *
```

**eiskaltdcpp-gtk / eiskaltdcpp-daemon**: same as neighboring files in that directory (GPL banner or `/*` block); keep prior authors and add Peter’s line.

## When to apply

- **Do** add on new feature modules and substantial new helpers extracted as their own translation units.
- **Do** add when splitting out new functionality the author owns; keep original copyrights on extracted legacy code and **append** Peter’s line if the new file is authored in this fork.
- **Do** keep the line when **moving/renaming** a module (`git mv`); if a move drops it, restore it at the new path.
- **Skip** generated files, pure include fragments (e.g. enum-only `.h` pulled into a class body), assets, and third-party trees (`json/`, vendored libs).
- **Skip** pre-existing upstream files that were only lightly edited; copyright belongs on modules introduced or substantially owned in this fork.
